- c102 (X6): l7-validation reports the probe wrong: The probe does not compile on either parent: ValidateUserModification returns *locale.LocalizedError, whose Error() method returns error (not string), so strings.Contains(msg, ...) is a type error and the test build fails before any assertion runs. The failure is the probe's, not the product's; the clause expects behaviour that is not exercised at all.
- c101 (X7): l4-readingtime reports the probe wrong: The probe's own extra control is wrong, not the product: it asserts that the rule "EntryTitle=^$" does not match the test entry, but the entry has an empty Title, so ^$ legitimately matches and the control fails on the fork (left) where the clause holds. L4.7 only says the EntryReadingTime behaviour holds with EntryReadingTime in effect; it says nothing about EntryTitle rules on an empty title. Rewritten as l4_7_entryreadingtime_variant_v2.sh with a non-matching title control.
- c127 (X8): r13-x5-repair reports the probe wrong: The probe never sends a valid CSRF token, so it says nothing about R13.3 ("POST ... with a valid CSRF token answers a redirect ... and afterwards every entry of that feed is read"). Its fixture new_unread_entry inserts entries straight into PostgreSQL without author, leaving author NULL; on both parents loading /unread then fails with `store: unable to fetch entry row: sql: Scan error on column index 8, name "author": converting NULL to string is unsupported` (HTTP 500) - reproduced locally on the left tree. Because `csrf=$(csrf_token_for_current_session)` is a command substitution, setup_fail only exits the subshell, so the probe carries on with an empty csrf and POSTs with no token; the CSRF middleware answers 400 and the category handler is never reached (logs/c127-{left,right}.log: "answered 400 instead of 302"). The failure is the probe's fixture/CSRF setup, not the right tree's behaviour.
- c268 (R14): scheduler-unlock reports the probe wrong: The probe does not actually supply a valid CSRF token on the left tree, so its left failure is a setup artifact, not the clause's behaviour. csrf_token_for_current_session() calls setup_fail inside a command substitution, so the exit only leaves the subshell; on left the /unread page has no name="csrf" field (left's common/layout.html lacks the hidden csrf input right's has), so csrf was empty and all three POSTs got 400 "invalid or missing CSRF". R14.4 says "Valid-CSRF POSTs ... answer 302 with Locations ...", so the probe must present a valid token; it should read the session token from a page that carries the field in both trees (/settings).
- c281 (X9): scheduler-unlock reports the probe wrong: The probe's fixture is not a shape the product produces: it inserts entries with published_at = now() and leaves feeds.checked_at NULL, while storage.MarkFeedAsRead only marks entries with published_at < checked_at (CheckedAt returns the feed's checked_at). So on the right tip the POST correctly answered 302 to /category/<id>/feeds but no entry could be marked read, and the probe's extra "entry is read" assertion failed on the owning tip, inventing repair X9. R13.6 only says the behaviour still holds after a clean restart; the fixture must date the entries before the feed's checked_at, as the existing R13.3 probe does.
- c481 (X5): orchestrator reports the probe wrong: The probe's cookie-format check contradicts X5.5 and R11's required "<id>.<secret>" cookie format. Lines 35-41 reject every value matching the shell pattern `*[!.]*`, which means "contains at least one non-dot character"; therefore every valid non-empty MinifluxSessionID such as the logged `I4FKTCNN7OBOFXSVNHHBMVGGD2.5QIVLHA5CUJREU34VFVXLA33AB` is treated as malformed. X5.5 requires the discussion request and refresh request to run on an authenticated session, not a cookie whose value contains only dots. The probe must instead require nonempty id and secret components (and then make both requests with that cookie).
- c498 (X10): x3-webpage-propose reports the probe wrong: The probe is broken, not the product: its shell greps still match '^X3\.4 ok '/'^X3\.4 bad ' (escaped dot, untouched by the rename) while the embedded analyzer prints 'R8.6 ok/bad', so ANALYSIS never yields a result and the probe reports "the structural analyzer produced no R8.6 result" on every tree. It therefore fails on right for a probe bug, not because right breaks R8.6 ("the feed refresh, article/icon scraping and subscription-discovery request builders pass the feed's own URL as the cookie site").
- c507 (X11): r7-uppercase-schemes reports the probe wrong: R7.4 says only that untrustedURL("HTTPS://example.org") and untrustedURL("SVN+SSH://example.org") "return their input unchanged". The probe additionally asserted an html/template href render equals `<a href="SVN+SSH://example.org">x</a>`, but html/template's URL-attribute escaping encodes '+' as '&#43;', so that assertion fails on right even though the function returns the input unchanged (the direct-call assertions passed there). The render assertion is not in the clause's words.
- c513 (X12): r15-entry-ids reports the probe wrong: The probe's own fixture expectation is wrong, not the product: the clause (R15.6) only says the client sends the set query fields and decodes {total,entry_ids}; the probe's "combined" case asserts {Limit:2,Starred:false,Status:read} yields total 1 with the read unstarred id, but its fixtures contain no read+unstarred entry (e1 is unread+unstarred, e3 is read+starred), so the correct answer is total 0 / []. The clause says nothing about that data combination; the probe must assert the values its own fixtures really imply.
- c560 (X15): r3-r4-gotify-retry reports the probe wrong: The probe drives the product with a loopback httptest server while INTEGRATION_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORKS is unset, so on the right tree the request is refused by the product's private-network guard ("client: connection to private network is blocked") before any Gotify behaviour is exercised. R4.6 says nothing about private-network policy; the probe must allow private networks (as the shared gotify harness does) so the clamp/validation behaviour is what is measured.
- c615 (X16): fix round 4 would have had to revert the other side's lines in internal/reader/filter/filter.go to make it pass, so the probe likely asserts one side's own behaviour; rewrite it from the clause text. The fix said: X16/L4.6 requires `EntryReadingTime=> 5` (whitespace between the operator and the value) to behave exactly like `>=5` and match ReadingTime 5, while L4.3 keeps the unspaced `EntryReadingTime=>5` stric
- c618 (X17): wave3-r15-5-http reports the probe wrong: Probe bug, not product: in check_unauthorized the label "$1" was never shifted away, so `api_get "/v1/entries/ids" "$@"` passed the words "unauthenticated"/"bogus-token" to curl as extra URLs ("curl: (6) Could not resolve host: unauthenticated"), producing HTTP_CODE="000...401" and a stale body. That makes the 401 clause fail on the right tip (owner) even though the authenticated 400 checks and the reach-the-endpoint control behave as the clause says there. The clause's own words (400 for ?starred=maybe and ?status=invalid, 401 unauthenticated) are unchanged; a rewritten probe does the same requests with the label argument consumed properly.

